FINAL ANSWER:
Troy Goode and Douglas Christopher Wilson own most of the code that is still here. Together they hold about 70% of the surviving lines.

I counted lines with `git blame -w -M -C` at HEAD, across 19 files. That is 1,664 non-blank lines (1,997 with blank lines), and 1,664 is the base for every percentage below.

| Author | Surviving lines | Share | Commits (share) |
|---|---|---|---|
| Troy Goode (2 addresses, see below) | 649 | 39.0% | 95 (30.1%) |
| Douglas Christopher Wilson | 551 | 33.1% | 143 (45.3%) |
| Íñigo Marquínez Prado | 80 | 4.8% | 2 (0.6%) |
| Jon Church | 59 | 3.5% | 3 (0.9%) |
| Sebastian Beltran | 57 | 3.4% | 2 (0.6%) |
| Ulises Gascón | 46 | 2.8% | 3 (0.9%) |
| Ignacio Mangas | 38 | 2.3% | 2 (0.6%) |
| Alejandro Caravaca Puchades | 30 | 1.8% | 5 (1.6%) |
| Linus Unnebäck | 26 | 1.6% | 1 (0.3%) |
| Ollie Buck | 14 | 0.8% | 1 (0.3%) |

52 more authors hold 6.9% together. The largest of them is Eric di Domenico at 0.7%.

**Ranking by lines instead of commits**
- Wilson has the most commits (45.3%) but only 33.1% of the surviving lines.
- Troy Goode has fewer commits (30.1%) but 39.0% of the lines. Most of the code that remains is his.
- Several people with only 2 or 3 commits (Marquínez Prado, Church, Beltran) still hold 3–5% each.

**Bus factor**
- The bus factor is 2. This is the smallest number of authors whose surviving lines together reach 50% of the lines counted. Goode and Wilson together hold 72.1% of the 1,664 non-blank lines.
- Reaching 80% takes 4 authors.
- Both figures cover the whole repository.

**Identities**
- **Troy Goode:** he appears under two addresses, `<email redacted>` (605 lines, 36.4%) and `troy@laptop.example` (44 lines, 2.6%). The 649-line figure above merges them. This is a guess from the matching name and similar addresses. You haven't confirmed it, and I wrote nothing to `.mailmap`. Unmerged, he is still first with 605 lines (36.4%) and Wilson is second with 551 (33.1%).
- **Alejandro Caravaca Puchades:** his address also appears under the name `alex94puchades`, and the tool already counts the two as one person. He holds 30 lines.

**Left out of the numbers**
- **Reformat commit:** `62628c0` ("style: reformat js with 4-space indent and double quotes") is listed in `.git-blame-ignore-revs`, so blame skips it. It rewrote 1,016 lines across `lib/index.js` and 3 test files. Without that exclusion, whoever ran the reformat would hold the lines it touched.
- **Bot:** `dependabot[bot]` is excluded. That is 10 lines and 11 commits, and none of it is credited to anyone.

**Mechanical rewrites**
- I checked for mechanical rewrites. The tool examined 141 commits and set aside none beyond the reformat above.
- It flagged 5 more commits that look mechanical and left them in the counts. The two largest are Wilson's `739dc11` ("docs: reformat license", 17 lines) and Goode's `fcd2b56` (the WIP ESLint switch, 14 lines).
- The other three are Wilson's ESLint and build commits, with 9, 1 and 1 surviving lines. None of them would change the ranking.

**Copy detection:** the figures use `-w -M -C`, which follows moves and copies between files. The tool reported no author whose share moved by more than 2 points because of it.

**What the numbers don't show:** code quality, review work, design done outside the code, or code that was deleted on purpose.

If you want the Goode merge made permanent, I can add the `.mailmap` line. The Caravaca Puchades line is already applied.

